WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:28.779
Picture the scene: it’s January 24, 41 AD
and Rome is in chaos.

00:00:28.779 --> 00:00:33.620
The tyrant emperor Caligula has just been
stabbed to death outside the Palatine Games.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:39.120
In the city, his wife has been murdered and
his baby daughter had her head dashed open

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.230
against a wall.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:44.200
In this tense, bloody atmosphere, no-one knows
what will happen next.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:49.649
Will the Republic be restored, or will a new
emperor be found to take the throne?

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:54.019
At the height of this uncertainty, a soldier
in the Praetorian Guard sweeps aside a palace

00:00:54.019 --> 00:00:58.500
curtain and finds Caligula’s useless uncle
cowering in fright.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.620
But the Guard don’t kill the old man.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:04.500
Instead they proclaim uncle Claudius Emperor.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.530
Born into the imperial household in the days
of Augustus, Claudius was a sick, feeble child

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:11.679
never destined to amount to much.

00:01:11.679 --> 00:01:17.500
Yet through a quirk of fate he became the
only candidate able to take the throne following

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:18.640
Caligula’s murder.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:23.670
Hounded by assassination attempts, surrounded
by people conspiring against him, Claudius

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:27.130
nonetheless became one of the greatest rulers
in Roman history.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:40.550
Join us today as we plunge into the unlikely
story of Rome’s accidental emperor.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:43.650
The Family of Blood
On August 1, 10 BC, the Roman town of Lugdunum

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:48.380
(now Lyon, France) prepared to welcome the
latest member of the imperial household into

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:49.380
the world.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.520
From the moment of his birth, Claudius - or,
to give him his full name, Tiberius Claudius

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.100
Nero Germanicus - was one of the best connected
people in the whole of Rome.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:04.240
His father was the popular general Nero Claudius
Drusus, while his mother was Antonia the Younger.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.479
If that doesn’t sound so impressive, wait
until you hear the next part.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:12.440
His grandmother on his father’s side was
the wife of the current emperor Augustus.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:16.230
On his mother’s side, he was descended from
Mark Anthony.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:19.090
Not only that, but his uncle was the future
emperor Tiberius.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:23.860
His older brother was Germanicus, the future
father of Caligula.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:27.060
Claudius wasn’t just born with a silver
spoon in his mouth.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:31.390
He was born practically suckling a whole cutlery
set.

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:34.760
Unfortunately, he was also born with a mysterious
disability.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.890
We say “unfortunately” because ancient
Rome was not somewhere that viewed the disabled

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:39.890
kindly.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:44.890
The Roman ideal was to be a strong male who
excelled at both athletic feats and oratory.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:47.819
Being able to do only one of those things
was acceptable.

00:02:47.819 --> 00:02:49.180
But neither?

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.370
Boy, would you be in for a bad time.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:55.959
And a bad time is exactly what young Claudius
had.

00:02:55.959 --> 00:02:56.959
He limped.

00:02:56.959 --> 00:02:57.959
He stammered.

00:02:57.959 --> 00:02:58.959
His head twitched oddly.

00:02:58.959 --> 00:03:00.290
He was known to dribble.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:05.330
For a family that prided itself on being the
most perfectly Roman of all Romans, this was

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:06.630
beyond embarrassing.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:11.750
Claudius’s mother called him “a monster
of a man, not finished but merely begun by

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:12.750
Dame Nature.”

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.870
His grandmother refused to speak with him.

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:18.450
It’s said that when poor Claudius came to
dinner, the rest of the family would throw

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:21.850
food at him and jeer, mocking him for his
disability.

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:26.170
If that sounds horrific, just know that Claudius
was one of the lucky ones.

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:29.890
In those days, it was not uncommon for Romans
to simply kill disabled children.

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:33.900
The only reason the imperial family didn’t
do so is because that would’ve been an even

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:35.340
bigger embarrassment.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:40.080
But while Claudius was disabled, he was by
no means the idiot his parents thought he

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:41.080
was.

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.190
The boy was insightful, intelligent.

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:44.190
A born academic.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:46.130
At first, no-one really noticed.

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:50.930
After Claudius’s father died in 9 BC he
was dumped with a tutor who tried to beat

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:52.630
his disability out of him.

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:57.790
But, eventually, Augustus noticed Claudius’s
love of reading and, in 7 AD, decided to hire

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:00.650
the historian Livy as his new tutor.

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:02.040
This was an excellent move.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:05.960
Livy didn’t try to beat Claudius until his
disability went away.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:10.570
He gave him stacks of books to read, encouraged
him to write, even helped him prepare and

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:11.680
deliver speeches.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:16.120
As young Claudius started to come out of his
shell, he began to earn begrudging respect

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:21.650
from his relatives - no mean feat when you
remember those relatives were all assholes.

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:26.370
Yet, even now, the imperial family kept Claudius
from holding any public office.

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:30.960
In private, they all agreed he should never,
ever be allowed to become emperor.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.720
You can almost imagine the Fates looking down
on these pompous mortals and struggling not

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:40.210
to laugh.

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:43.690
From Bad…

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:47.910
The tortured chain of events that would eventually
lead to Claudius becoming emperor began on

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:49.820
19 August, 14 AD.

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:53.560
That day, Augustus passed away after four
decades on the throne.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:58.270
In the wake of the First Citizen’s death,
Claudius’s uncle Tiberius became emperor.

00:04:58.270 --> 00:04:59.940
Now, Tiberius was a weird choice.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:07.350
He was about as popular as a rectally inserted
cactus, and had only been adopted by Augustus

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:09.650
because he had no other heirs.

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:14.280
Perhaps realizing that Emperor Butt-Cactus
would be a disaster, Augustus had tried to

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:20.870
mitigate things by making Tiberius in turn
adopt Claudius’s dashing older brother Germanicus.

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:26.090
That way, no matter how painful Tiberius was
for Rome’s metaphorical backside, the soothing

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:30.000
hemorrhoid cream of Germanicus would never
be far behind.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.750
Unfortunately, Tiberius was a guy who believed
in all pain, no gain.

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:38.669
In 19 AD, Tiberius had Germanicus assassinated.

00:05:38.669 --> 00:05:43.090
In the aftermath, he had all of Germanicus’s
relatives either killed or imprisoned.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:44.610
Well, we say “all”.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:51.310
But there were two conspicuous relatives this
sentient, spiky suppository forgot about.

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:56.580
The first was Claudius, who Tiberius considered
too weak and useless to even bother killing.

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:01.320
The second was Germanicus’s son: a pre-teen
boy who went by the nickname Caligula.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:04.889
And that was basically how the rest of Tiberius’s
reign went.

00:06:04.889 --> 00:06:09.240
Throughout the 20s AD, the emperor conducted
purge after purge.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:13.340
Each time, he spared both useless Claudius
and helpless Caligula.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:16.630
That’s not to say Tiberius liked his stammering
nephew.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:20.300
He kept Claudius alive because he didn’t
see him as a threat, but also made sure to

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:21.510
keep him miserable.

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:25.560
When the senate tried to let the poor guy
join, Tiberius personally vetoed it for the

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:26.639
good of the empire.

00:06:26.639 --> 00:06:31.330
On the other hand, the emperor also made a
choice that was very much not for the good

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:32.330
of the empire.

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:35.370
In 32 AD, Tiberius was reaching the end of
his life and needed an heir.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:39.431
His son Drusus had died the decade before,
and he needed someone to fill the boy’s

00:06:39.431 --> 00:06:40.431
little boots.

00:06:40.431 --> 00:06:41.979
He chose Caligula.

00:06:41.979 --> 00:06:47.020
Technically, he made Caligula his co-heir
alongside Caligula’s cousin, but you don’t

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:51.020
need to bother learning heir number 2’s
name, because Caligula quickly made sure their

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:53.530
was no second heir to worry about.

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:58.740
That done, he smothered Tiberius with a pillow
in 37 AD - at least, according to tradition

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:01.180
- and had himself declared sole emperor.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:06.160
Since you’ve doubtless already seen our
Caligula video - and, if not, do we have a

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:11.111
glorious Roman clickhole lined up for you
- you already know that Caligula’s reign

00:07:11.111 --> 00:07:15.080
was extremely not-fun for everyone in Rome.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:16.320
But not for Claudius.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.780
Almost as soon as Caligula took the throne,
he had his uncle elevated to consul, the first

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:24.330
position of power the now-47 year old had
held.

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:28.810
To this day, nobody really knows why Caligula
showed Claudius such favor.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:34.130
Theories range from “he wanted to keep his
potential enemies close” to “dude, he’s

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:36.449
Caligula, he’s completely cuckoo.”

00:07:36.449 --> 00:07:41.960
Yet even now a dark cloud was forming that
threatened to block Claudius’s unexpected

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:43.040
ray of sunshine.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:48.120
In December that year, Caligula’s sister
Agrippina the Younger gave birth to a son.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:51.530
She called him Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus.

00:07:51.530 --> 00:08:01.509
But you know him by a different name: Nero.
… to Worse

00:08:01.509 --> 00:08:05.130
While Caligula had taken a shine to Claudius,
that didn’t mean the older man was safe

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:06.449
from his nephew’s whims.

00:08:06.449 --> 00:08:11.270
By all accounts, Caligula treated Claudius
more like a court jester than a relative,

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:15.880
pushing him in the river for fun and forcing
the poor guy to rack up huge debts funding

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:17.100
Caligula’s lifestyle.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:21.930
Still, considering what happened to most people
in Caligula’s orbit, Claudius got off relatively

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:22.930
lightly.

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:26.900
This was the emperor who forced parents to
watch their children being tortured to death.

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:32.399
The emperor who banished his own sister, Claudius’s
niece Agrippina the Younger, to a remote island

00:08:32.399 --> 00:08:34.880
where she had to catch sponges for a living.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:37.070
By the way: Agrippina the Younger?

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:38.070
Remember that name.

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:43.529
Anyway, Claudius’s life under Caligula continued
to be a weird mix of awful and kinda OK.

00:08:43.529 --> 00:08:49.279
In 38 AD, he married Valeria Messalina, an
influential member of the court, and, in 39

00:08:49.279 --> 00:08:52.709
AD, the couple had a daughter: Claudia Octavia.

00:08:52.709 --> 00:08:54.720
Yet this wasn’t exactly true love.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:58.830
Messalina married Claudius because he was
her ticket into the imperial household.

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:03.500
The moment they’d tied the knot she systematically
began committing adultery with everything

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:04.500
with a pulse.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:10.000
The fact he was being cuckolded on an almost
hourly basis only added to Rome’s general

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.860
impression that Claudius was comically useless.

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:17.690
But it was this comical uselessness that made
Caligula treat his uncle more like a pet than

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:18.690
a rival.

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:22.940
And it wouldn’t be long before the poor,
abused dog bit back at its master.

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:25.980
By 41 AD, the knives were out for the mad
emperor.

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:27.680
In this case, literally.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:31.510
On January 24, Caligula was stabbed to death
outside the Palatine Games.

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:34.610
In the city, his wife and daughter were assassinated
in their home.

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:39.290
But the conspirators had been so focused on
killing Caligula that they hadn’t planned

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:40.610
for what came next.

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:43.940
The result was that Rome slid into near anarchy
that day.

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:47.700
In the senate, emergency debates were even
held on restoring the Republic.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:49.810
For the Praetorian Guard, this was worrying.

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:53.270
The private guard of the imperial household,
they effectively got all their power from

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:54.500
the system of emperors.

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:57.140
Revert back to a republic, and they were done
for.

00:09:57.140 --> 00:09:59.010
What they needed was a new emperor.

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:01.880
Someone who could take Caligula’s place
on the throne.

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:03.220
Ideally a relative.

00:10:03.220 --> 00:10:06.390
Ideally someone weak, who could be manipulated.

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:09.370
They found their candidate in the most unlikely
place.

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:12.910
At the moment the bloodshed erupted, Claudius
had been in the imperial palace.

00:10:12.910 --> 00:10:15.360
Fearing he might be next, he’d hidden behind
a curtain.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:20.990
And there he was still cowering when a Praetorian
Guardsman yanked it back, looking for all

00:10:20.990 --> 00:10:23.760
the world like he was going to stab the old
man.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:25.530
But there was no more bloodshed that day.

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:27.450
No murder of Caligula’s uncle.

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:33.330
Instead, Claudius watched in dumbfounded silence
as the Praetorian Guard knelt and hailed him

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:34.330
as their new emperor.

00:10:34.330 --> 00:10:38.440
Now, powerful as they were, the Praetorian
Guard couldn’t just unilaterally declare

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:39.470
Claudius emperor.

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:43.760
If the senate had really wanted to restore
the republic, there would’ve been very little

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:47.330
all these guys now kneeling around a curtain
could’ve done about it.

00:10:47.330 --> 00:10:48.950
But the senate was too divided.

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:53.220
So when the Praetorian Guard presented their
candidate, there was no united force to stop

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:54.220
them.

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:57.430
The day Claudius was declared emperor, you
could almost hear the city groan.

00:10:57.430 --> 00:10:58.430
Really?

00:10:58.430 --> 00:10:59.430
This idiot?

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:01.310
But Claudius wasn’t an idiot.

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:05.060
These grumbling Romans didn’t know it yet,
but “doddering” Claudius was going to

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:10.040
become the greatest emperor since Augustus.

00:11:10.040 --> 00:12:04.380
Stepping Stones

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:36.470
to Greatness
If you’d been

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:40.970
a Roman citizen in the early days of Claudius’s
rule, you probably would’ve felt like things

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:42.680
were off to a bad start.

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:46.810
One of Claudius’s first acts was to bung
the military a hefty bribe to stop them rebelling

00:12:46.810 --> 00:12:47.810
against him.

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:52.029
But that still wasn’t enough, because in
42 AD the governor of Dalmatia decided to

00:12:52.029 --> 00:12:54.250
go into rebellion regardless!

00:12:54.250 --> 00:12:57.980
This was pretty embarrassing for Claudius,
especially as some senators sided with the

00:12:57.980 --> 00:13:00.269
rebel governor, but it wasn’t fatal.

00:13:00.269 --> 00:13:04.540
At the last moment, the governor’s troops
rebelled against him and the would-be revolutionary

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:05.959
was forced to commit suicide.

00:13:05.959 --> 00:13:10.180
Still, it’s not like Claudius could crack
down on the dissenters or anything.

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:12.160
In this period, the senate was calling the
shots.

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:16.370
They declared that Caligula’s statues should
be torn down, and they mostly were.

00:13:16.370 --> 00:13:20.490
They blocked Claudius’s attempts to bring
Caligula’s assassins to justice, allowing

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:23.110
only the head conspirator to be executed.

00:13:23.110 --> 00:13:26.730
By the way, if you’re wondering why Claudius
went after the guys who did him a favor by

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:30.450
icing Caligula, it was mostly about precedent.

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:32.529
Everyone had benefited from Caligula dying.

00:13:32.529 --> 00:13:37.260
But you couldn’t have people thinking that
assassination was a legit way to remove an

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:38.260
emperor.

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:40.470
I mean, this was Ancient Rome, not Ancient
Ro- oh right.

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:41.470
Well, nevermind.

00:13:41.470 --> 00:13:45.649
The other big mistake Claudius made in his
early reign was, oddly, to show mercy.

00:13:45.649 --> 00:13:46.720
Remember Agrippina the Younger?

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:51.660
Caligula’s sister, Claudius’s niece, and
Nero’s mother who got exiled to that random

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:52.660
island?

00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:54.459
Well, Claudius brought her back to Rome.

00:13:54.459 --> 00:13:55.459
Pardoned her.

00:13:55.459 --> 00:13:58.970
Set her up with a new husband to look after
both her and Nero.

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:02.510
In the long run, this would prove to be a
fatal move.

00:14:02.510 --> 00:14:07.700
By the end of 42 AD Claudius was so insecure
in his position that it was entirely possible

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:11.320
there would be yet another new emperor within
the year.

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:13.130
What Claudius needed was a major victory.

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:18.500
A military conquest that would show the world
he was more than just a doddering old man.

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:20.680
Luckily, he had just the place in mind.

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:25.820
Nearly a century ago, the great Julius Caesar
had failed to conquer a rain-soaked island

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:26.820
north of Gaul.

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:29.010
That island’s name was Britannia.

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:31.959
And Claudius was gonna bring it into the Roman
empire.

00:14:31.959 --> 00:14:37.139
There’s a lot we don’t know about Claudius’s
conquest of Britannia in 43 AD.

00:14:37.139 --> 00:14:42.529
Our only real source is Cassius Dio, who was
born over 100 years after the invasion.

00:14:42.529 --> 00:14:47.639
But we do know that there were two major battles
with the Britons, finally leading to the establishment

00:14:47.639 --> 00:14:49.600
of a crossing point on the Thames.

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:53.580
We also know that Claudius personally went
to Britannia just in time to lead his troops

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:57.260
to victory, a move that won him major plaudits
back in Rome.

00:14:57.260 --> 00:15:00.920
We know, too, that he wasn’t the only emperor
involved in the war.

00:15:00.920 --> 00:15:04.769
The future emperor Vespasian was among the
commanders in Claudius’s army.

00:15:04.769 --> 00:15:10.220
On his own initiative he conquered the southwest
of Britain all the way to modern-day Exeter.

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:14.150
By the end of 43 AD, Claudius had done what
even Julius Caesar couldn’t.

00:15:14.150 --> 00:15:16.760
He’d pacified the south of this rainy island.

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:19.950
While there would be trouble with the Britons
in the future - Boudicca launched her famous

00:15:19.950 --> 00:15:24.180
revolt in 60 AD - for now, Claudius was riding
high.

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:26.090
Back in Rome, Claudius was awarded a triumph.

00:15:26.090 --> 00:15:30.740
For the first time, the average Roman in the
street was looking askance at the emperor

00:15:30.740 --> 00:15:34.139
and saying “hey, this dude ain’t so bad.”

00:15:34.139 --> 00:15:38.260
Well buckle up, ancient Romans, because, for
a while at least, things are gonna keep getting

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:39.850
better.

00:15:39.850 --> 00:15:47.380
Changing Attitudes
Today, Claudius’s reign is best remembered

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:52.180
for territorial expansion and for the fact
he wasn’t either Caligula or Nero.

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:56.959
But there were domestic triumphs, too, and
they went beyond merely building aqueducts.

00:15:56.959 --> 00:16:00.779
Claudius was the first emperor since Augustus
to really shake up the bureaucracy.

00:16:00.779 --> 00:16:05.029
While Tiberius had contented himself with
the odd purge, and Caligula had just done

00:16:05.029 --> 00:16:09.860
whatever wacky stuff popped into his mind,
Claudius tried to seriously reform the empire.

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:13.630
One of his key achievements was to introduce
Gauls into the Senate.

00:16:13.630 --> 00:16:17.110
Gaul had been under Roman rule for the best
part of a century, but the Senate was still

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:19.959
reserved for those born on the Italian peninsula.

00:16:19.959 --> 00:16:24.500
That was all fine and dandy when you were
a small conquering force of Italian groups,

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:28.790
but when you were a vast multinational empire,
it was less than ideal.

00:16:28.790 --> 00:16:33.639
Claudius was the one who broke the taboo on
letting non-Italians into the lawmaking process.

00:16:33.639 --> 00:16:37.540
Although it caused a massive fight at the
time, it likely saved the empire in the long

00:16:37.540 --> 00:16:38.540
run.

00:16:38.540 --> 00:16:42.290
Claudius also championed the rights of slaves
and ex-slaves, bringing in a law that made

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:44.820
it illegal to kill your slave for no reason.

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:48.670
To modern ears that might sound like one of
those things that should really go without

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:52.740
saying, but before Claudius there had been
nothing to stop a Roman citizen coming home

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:56.160
from a bad day at the forum and stabbing their
slave to death.

00:16:56.160 --> 00:17:01.230
In tandem with this decree, Claudius began
promoting freedmen inside his own court.

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:05.439
He made ex-slaves head of his correspondence,
head of the palace treasury, and placed them

00:17:05.439 --> 00:17:07.870
in other positions too numerous to count.

00:17:07.870 --> 00:17:10.630
For the senate, this was just another outrage.

00:17:10.630 --> 00:17:13.589
These guys were former slaves, the lowest
of the low!

00:17:13.589 --> 00:17:16.709
And now here they were, among the emperor’s
closest advisors!

00:17:16.709 --> 00:17:20.750
Perhaps its no surprise malicious rumors began
to spread that the freedmen were really the

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:23.750
ones controlling Claudius, and not the other
way around.

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:28.490
Not that Claudius was a perfect ruler, even
if you agreed with all this stuff he was doing.

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:33.039
He was prone to issuing odd decrees, such
as the ruling he passed that farting in public

00:17:33.039 --> 00:17:35.129
was good for you.

00:17:35.129 --> 00:17:36.309
Emperor Claudius, guys.

00:17:36.309 --> 00:17:38.960
History’s first proponent of “better out
than in”.

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:42.970
Overall, though, you’d be hard pressed as
an ancient Roman to say that Claudius was

00:17:42.970 --> 00:17:44.110
doing a bad job.

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:50.340
Oh, sure, the senate stank of imperial butt
gas half the time now, but stuff was otherwise

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:51.919
measuarbly better.

00:17:51.919 --> 00:17:55.299
Finances were again on a sound footing after
Caligula’s excess.

00:17:55.299 --> 00:18:00.869
The judiciary was functioning well, with Claudius
even personally hearing cases and granting

00:18:00.869 --> 00:18:04.700
clemency where the law demanded unduly harsh
punishment.

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:10.289
Yet despite all these undoubted improvements,
the Roman elites just could not get over the

00:18:10.289 --> 00:18:14.309
fact that Claudius was… well, Claudius.

00:18:14.309 --> 00:18:15.309
He still limped.

00:18:15.309 --> 00:18:16.309
He still stammered.

00:18:16.309 --> 00:18:17.700
His wife still cheated on him.

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:21.740
And, for the toxically macho Romans, that
was simply too much.

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:25.909
In his years in charge, Claudius was forced
to put over 300 people to death for plotting

00:18:25.909 --> 00:18:26.950
against him.

00:18:26.950 --> 00:18:31.559
Pretty much every week brought some new assassination
plot or planned rebellion.

00:18:31.559 --> 00:18:36.710
Yet Claudius dodged them all, wandering obliviously
through the drawn daggers like Buster Keaton

00:18:36.710 --> 00:18:37.889
in a hurricane.

00:18:37.889 --> 00:18:41.690
But while Claudius was becoming an expert
at avoiding assassination attempts, there

00:18:41.690 --> 00:18:49.159
was one he wouldn’t see coming until it
was far too late.

00:18:49.159 --> 00:18:54.289
Goodbye, Uncle
In 47 AD, Agrippina the Younger’s wealthy

00:18:54.289 --> 00:18:59.019
husband dropped dead in mysterious circumstances,
leaving the emperor’s niece in need of a

00:18:59.019 --> 00:19:00.019
new man.

00:19:00.019 --> 00:19:04.129
This was fortunate timing, because the emperor
was about to be in the market for a new wife.

00:19:04.129 --> 00:19:08.679
OK, so remember Claudius’s wife Messalina,
the one who kept cheating on him?

00:19:08.679 --> 00:19:12.999
Well, she was still cheating on him like she
was trying to break some sort of record.

00:19:12.999 --> 00:19:14.590
Which she possibly was.

00:19:14.590 --> 00:19:19.159
It’s said Messalina and a famous Roman prostitute
had a competition to see who could sleep with

00:19:19.159 --> 00:19:21.669
the most men in a single day.

00:19:21.669 --> 00:19:22.730
Messalina won.

00:19:22.730 --> 00:19:27.109
But Messalina’s plans went further than
just getting her kicks behind Claudius’s

00:19:27.109 --> 00:19:28.109
back.

00:19:28.109 --> 00:19:32.210
Like everyone else, she was still convinced
the emperor was an idiot, to the extent that

00:19:32.210 --> 00:19:36.820
she actually married another man in a lavish
ceremony in the heart of Rome.

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:40.500
The plan was that she would kill Claudius
and her new husband assume the throne.

00:19:40.500 --> 00:19:44.039
Instead, word of this incredibly not-secret
secret marriage got back to the emperor, who

00:19:44.039 --> 00:19:45.730
was all like “wait, what?

00:19:45.730 --> 00:19:48.950
That’s a stupid plan” and had Messalina
killed.

00:19:48.950 --> 00:19:53.889
And so it was that Claudius became single
again just as Agrippina was looking for a

00:19:53.889 --> 00:19:54.960
new squeeze.

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:59.749
Now, if you’ve already seen our Nero video
- and, yes, we did these emperors out of order.

00:19:59.749 --> 00:20:03.289
Just trying to keep you on your toes - you’ll
know Agrippina was a woman desperate to secure

00:20:03.289 --> 00:20:05.149
herself against future poverty.

00:20:05.149 --> 00:20:09.090
However, the way she went about it was all
sorts of eww.

00:20:09.090 --> 00:20:14.499
Throughout 48 AD, Agrippina set her sights
on seducing her uncle Claudius.

00:20:14.499 --> 00:20:20.190
Of course, it takes two to tango, and Claudius
was just as creepily open to the suggestion

00:20:20.190 --> 00:20:21.679
as Agrippina was.

00:20:21.679 --> 00:20:26.580
When they got married on New Year’s Day,
49 AD, the official reason was to unite the

00:20:26.580 --> 00:20:28.690
two branches of the imperial family.

00:20:28.690 --> 00:20:32.729
But Roman society at large was pretty sure
it was just a convenient excuse for Claudius

00:20:32.729 --> 00:20:35.830
to live out his weird incest fantasies.

00:20:35.830 --> 00:20:38.440
But not everyone saw the marriage as a PR
disaster.

00:20:38.440 --> 00:20:43.840
For the past decade, young Nero had been living
with his mother, the two quietly biding their

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:44.840
time.

00:20:44.840 --> 00:20:45.840
Now, that time had come.

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:49.450
In 50 AD, Agrippina convinced Claudius to
officially adopt Nero as his son.

00:20:49.450 --> 00:20:50.582
A year later, she pressured the emperor into
naming Nero his heir.

00:20:50.582 --> 00:20:54.749
Now, Nero was technically co-heir, alongside
Claudius’s previous son Britannicus.

00:20:54.749 --> 00:20:58.049
But that hadn’t stopped Caligula from seizing
power alone.

00:20:58.049 --> 00:21:00.070
And it wouldn’t stop Nero either.

00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:06.110
The last act came in 53 AD, when Agrippina
made Nero marry Claudius’s daughter, Claudia

00:21:06.110 --> 00:21:07.159
Octavia.

00:21:07.159 --> 00:21:12.190
From having nothing just a few years beforehand,
Agrippina and Nero had maneuvered their way

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:13.840
into the heart of power.

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:15.100
She was the emperor’s wife.

00:21:15.100 --> 00:21:19.549
He was the emperor’s adopted son and heir,
and married to the emperor’s daughter.

00:21:19.549 --> 00:21:23.909
It should’ve been blindingly obvious to
anyone watching that Agrippina and the teenage

00:21:23.909 --> 00:21:26.979
Nero were preparing for a hostile takeover.

00:21:26.979 --> 00:21:32.450
But, as he had been with Messalina, Claudius
was blind to the flaws of his new wife.

00:21:32.450 --> 00:21:36.169
Oblivious to the danger he was in, it’s
likely he never even realized what was happening.

00:21:36.169 --> 00:21:40.070
Agrippina and Nero made their move on October
13, 54 AD.

00:21:40.070 --> 00:21:43.820
That day, Claudius abruptly died from a sudden
sickness.

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:48.649
While it’s not certain what happened, tradition
holds that Agrippina bribed a court taster

00:21:48.649 --> 00:21:51.269
to slip Claudius poisonous mushrooms.

00:21:51.269 --> 00:21:55.320
Whatever the truth, the moment the emperor
was dead, Agrippina led Nero to the Praetorian

00:21:55.320 --> 00:21:58.349
Guard and had him declared sole ruler of Rome.

00:21:58.349 --> 00:21:59.379
And that was that.

00:21:59.379 --> 00:22:03.580
The reign of Claudius was over, and the time
of Nero was here.

00:22:03.580 --> 00:22:05.110
And what a time it would be!

00:22:05.110 --> 00:22:09.190
As he grew older, Nero would get crazier and
crazier until he finally…!

00:22:09.190 --> 00:22:14.729
But you’ll have to watch our Nero video
for the rest of that story.

00:22:14.729 --> 00:22:19.659
As for Claudius, he was deified after his
death, worshipped as a God by his former subjects.

00:22:19.659 --> 00:22:24.299
Compared to the fates of Tiberius, Caligula,
and Nero, this was a bit of a win.

00:22:24.299 --> 00:22:29.710
Even Nero revoking Claudius’s deification
in the 60s AD didn’t dent the accidental

00:22:29.710 --> 00:22:30.720
emperor’s popularity.

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:35.379
A few years later, Vespasian would restore
Claudius to his place in the pantheon.

00:22:35.379 --> 00:22:39.090
At the end of all that excitement, then, what
should we make of Claudius?

00:22:39.090 --> 00:22:41.519
Well, from one angle, he was still a bit of
a disaster.

00:22:41.519 --> 00:22:46.519
Don’t forget: without Claudius marrying
Agrippina, you don’t get Nero, arguably

00:22:46.519 --> 00:22:47.700
the worst emperor of all.

00:22:47.700 --> 00:22:51.720
But, to look at it from another angle, his
story is one of inspiration.

00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:57.679
Here was a man born disabled into a society
that routinely killed its disabled members,

00:22:57.679 --> 00:23:00.289
only to rise to the very top.

00:23:00.289 --> 00:23:04.359
Once he reached the top, he ruled with a sense
of moderation and sanity not seen since the

00:23:04.359 --> 00:23:05.830
days of Augustus.

00:23:05.830 --> 00:23:07.820
He made life in the empire fairer.

00:23:07.820 --> 00:23:12.769
He saw his subjects not as toys he could break
on a whim, but as actual people.

00:23:12.769 --> 00:23:17.559
In the end, Claudius may not have been a truly
great emperor like, say, Trajan.

00:23:17.559 --> 00:23:19.549
But he was a good man.

00:23:19.549 --> 00:23:21.529
Decent, kind.

00:23:21.529 --> 00:23:23.509
Not cruel.

00:23:23.509 --> 00:23:26.390
Everything his successor would fail to be.

00:23:26.390 --> 00:23:42.539
Perhaps that fact alone is enough to justify
his place in history.

